<h3>Strategy 6: Video SEO for "Near Me" Searches</h3>

<p>YouTube is the second-largest search engine, and "how to" + service searches are growing 23% year-over-year. A roofing contractor in Tampa started creating 3-5 minute videos answering common questions: "How to Spot Roof Damage After a Storm," "What Happens During a Roof Inspection," and "How Long Does Roof Replacement Take?"</p>

<p>He embedded these videos on his service pages and optimized the YouTube titles with local keywords. Within four months, his videos were appearing in Google's video carousel for "roofing contractor Tampa" and related searches. His website traffic from YouTube increased 340%, and he reported that video-watching leads converted at 28% higher rates than non-video leads because they arrived pre-educated.</p>

<p><strong>Short answer:</strong> The most effective SEO strategy for home service lead generation is building hyper-local service pages combined with transparent pricing content and an aggressive review generation system. These three elements create topical authority, answer the questions homeowners actually search for, and signal trust to both Google and potential customers.</p>

<h2>PPC Strategies: Buying Intent Traffic at the Right Price</h2>

<p>Pay-per-click advertising remains the fastest way to generate leads for home service contractors. You can launch a campaign today and have booked appointments tomorrow. The problem: most contractors are overpaying for clicks that don't convert and underinvesting in the campaigns that actually work.</p>

<h3>Strategy 7: Google Local Service Ads (LSA) Optimization</h3>

<p>Google Local Service Ads have become the highest-intent, lowest-cost lead source for many contractors. Unlike traditional Google Ads, you pay per lead, not per click. And the leads come pre-qualified: homeowners have already selected their service, location, and preferred time.</p>

<p>But most contractors set up their LSA profile once and never optimize it. The top performers treat LSA like a campaign that needs constant tuning.</p>

<p>Here's the optimization framework from a PPC specialist who manages $400K/month in home service ad spend:</p>

<ol>
<li><strong>Maximize your review count on LSA specifically.</strong> LSA displays your Google review count and rating prominently. Contractors with 100+ reviews and a 4.8+ rating see 40-60% lower cost per lead than those with fewer reviews.</li>
<li><strong>Use every service category available.</strong> Google allows you to select multiple service types. Select all that apply, even if they're secondary services. A plumber who only selected "drain cleaning" and "water heater repair" was missing leads for "pipe repair," "sewer line replacement," and "fixture installation." Adding those categories increased his LSA lead volume by 67%.</li>
<li><strong>Set your budget high enough to stay in the top 3.</strong> LSA uses a ranking system. Contractors in positions 1-3 get 70% of the leads. If your budget is too low to maintain top-3 placement during peak hours, you're invisible when demand is highest.</li>
<li><strong>Dispute every bad lead aggressively.</strong> Google credits you for leads that are outside your service area, wrong service type, or spam. Contractors who dispute systematically reduce their effective cost per lead by 15-25%.</li>
</ol>

<p>An HVAC company in Houston reduced their LSA cost per lead from $87 to $41 by implementing these four tactics over 90 days. Their monthly lead volume increased from 34 to 71.</p>

<h3>Strategy 8: Search Campaign Structure for Home Services</h3>

<p>Traditional Google Search campaigns still work, but the structure matters more than ever. The old approach — one campaign with a few broad keywords — bleeds budget on irrelevant clicks.</p>

<p>The campaign structure that wins in 2026:</p>

<ul>
<li><strong>Campaign 1: Brand</strong> — Your company name and variations. Low cost, high conversion. Never let competitors bid on your brand without fighting back.</li>
<li><strong>Campaign 2: Emergency/24-Hour</strong> — Keywords like "emergency plumber near me," "24 hour HVAC repair." These have the highest intent and highest cost per click, but they also have the highest close rate (often 60-80%).</li>
<li><strong>Campaign 3: Service-Specific</strong> — Individual campaigns for each major service: water heater replacement, drain cleaning, AC installation, etc. Each with its own ad group, landing page, and negative keyword list.</li>
<li><strong>Campaign 4: Competitor</strong> — Bid on competitor brand names (where legally allowed). Lower conversion rate, but the leads are actively shopping.</li>
<li><strong>Campaign 5: Remarketing</strong> — Target past website visitors who didn't convert. These campaigns typically deliver 3-5x return on ad spend because the audience already knows you.</li>
</ul>

<p>A roofing contractor in Seattle using this structure saw their cost per lead drop from $145 to $68 in four months. The key was separating emergency keywords (which converted at 22%) from general roofing keywords (which converted at 4%). Without separate campaigns, the budget was being consumed by low-intent clicks.</p>

