<h3>Strategy 10: Negative Keyword Discipline</h3>

<p>The fastest way to waste PPC budget is to show ads for searches that will never convert. A plumber paying for clicks from people searching "plumber salary," "how to become a plumber," or "plumber tools" is burning money.</p>

<p>Every home service PPC account should have a robust negative keyword list that includes:</p>

<ul>
<li>Job/career terms: salary, jobs, career, training, school, apprentice</li>
<li>DIY terms: how to, DIY, repair yourself, fix yourself, tutorial</li>
<li>Free/cheap terms: free, cheap, discount, coupon (unless you run promotions)</li>
<li>Competitor terms: if you're not running competitor campaigns</li>
<li>Location mismatches: cities outside your service area</li>
<li>Irrelevant services: if you don't offer commercial, add "commercial" as negative</li>
</ul>

<p>A pest control company in Atlanta audited their search terms report and found 34% of their clicks were coming from irrelevant searches. Adding 200+ negative keywords reduced their cost per lead by 41% while maintaining the same lead volume.</p>

<h3>Strategy 11: Call Tracking and Attribution</h3>

<p>You can't optimize what you don't measure. Every home service PPC campaign needs call tracking that attributes phone calls to specific ads, keywords, and landing pages.</p>

<p>Without call tracking, you're flying blind. A contractor might see 50 form submissions and think their campaign is working, while missing that 80% of their leads actually come from phone calls. Or worse, they might be paying $200 per lead for one campaign while another campaign delivers $40 leads, and they have no idea because everything is lumped together.</p>

<p>The minimum viable tracking setup:</p>

<ol>
<li>Unique phone numbers for each campaign (Google Ads, Facebook, organic, direct mail)</li>
<li>Call recording to assess lead quality and sales team performance</li>
<li>CRM integration so every lead is tracked from click to closed job</li>
<li>Revenue attribution so you know which campaigns generate profit, not just leads</li>
</ol>

<p>An HVAC company in Orlando discovered through call tracking that their "AC repair" campaign generated leads that closed at 18%, while their "AC maintenance" campaign generated leads that closed at 42%. They reallocated budget toward maintenance and increased their return on ad spend by 210%.</p>

<h3>Strategy 12: Facebook and Instagram Ads for Home Services</h3>

<p>Facebook and Instagram ads don't capture intent like Google Ads do. Nobody scrolls Instagram thinking "I need a new water heater." But that's exactly why they work for certain home service strategies.</p>

<p>The three Facebook ad strategies that generate leads for contractors:</p>

<ul>
<li><strong>Seasonal awareness campaigns:</strong> Before summer, run ads about AC tune-ups. Before winter, run ads about furnace inspections. You're creating demand before the emergency happens. A furnace company in Minneapolis ran pre-winter awareness ads in September and October. When the first cold snap hit in November, their phone rang off the hook because homeowners had already seen their brand.</li>
<li><strong>Retargeting website visitors:</strong> Someone visits your site, doesn't convert, and sees your ad on Facebook the next day. Simple, effective, and typically delivers 5-10x ROAS. A roofing contractor in Denver retargeted website visitors with a video ad showing a recent roof replacement. The campaign generated 23 leads at $31 per lead.</li>
<li><strong>Lookalike audiences from customers:</strong> Upload your customer list to Facebook and create a lookalike audience. Target homeowners who match the demographics and behaviors of your best customers. A plumbing company in San Diego used this approach and reduced their cost per lead from $78 to $44.</li>
</ul>

<p><strong>Short answer:</strong> The most effective PPC strategy for home services is optimizing Google Local Service Ads for maximum visibility while building tightly structured Search campaigns with dedicated landing pages and rigorous negative keyword management. Add call tracking for attribution, and you'll know exactly which campaigns generate profit.</p>

