<h2>Implementation Guide: Your 90-Day Lead Generation Action Plan</h2>

<p>Strategy without execution is just theory. Here's a step-by-step plan to implement the highest-impact lead generation tactics over the next 90 days.</p>

<h3>Days 1-30: Foundation</h3>

<p>Week 1: Audit and fix your Google Business Profile. Add all services, update photos, verify information, and start posting weekly.</p>

<p>Week 2: Implement a review generation system. Create the text message template, train technicians on the ask, and set up automated follow-up emails.</p>

<p>Week 3: Audit your website for technical issues. Check mobile speed, fix broken forms, add schema markup, and ensure all pages are indexable.</p>

<p>Week 4: Set up call tracking. Get unique numbers for each channel, implement call recording, and integrate with your CRM.</p>

<h3>Days 31-60: Acceleration</h3>

<p>Week 5: Launch or optimize Google Local Service Ads. Maximize your profile, set competitive budgets, and start disputing bad leads.</p>

<p>Week 6: Restructure Google Search campaigns. Separate by intent (emergency vs. general), create dedicated landing pages, and build your negative keyword list.</p>

<p>Week 7: Create your first transparent pricing content piece. Pick your highest-volume service and publish a detailed pricing guide.</p>

<p>Week 8: Build your referral system. Create the script, set up tracking, and launch with your next 10 customers.</p>

<h3>Days 61-90: Amplification</h3>

<p>Week 9: Start a content calendar. Plan 12 pieces of seasonal content aligned with your demand cycles.</p>

<p>Week 10: Launch social media content series. Pick one platform (Instagram, TikTok, or Facebook) and commit to 3-5 posts per week.</p>

<p>Week 11: Create a lead magnet. Build a cost calculator, checklist, or comparison guide and add it to your highest-traffic pages.</p>

<p>Week 12: Measure, analyze, and optimize. Review your data, identify what's working, and double down on your top-performing channels.</p>

<h3>The Daily Habits That Drive Results</h3>

<p>Beyond the 90-day plan, the contractors generating the most leads have daily habits:</p>

<ul>
<li><strong>Respond to leads within 5 minutes.</strong> A study by MIT found that contacting a lead within 5 minutes makes you 100x more likely to reach them than waiting 30 minutes. Speed to lead is the highest-leverage habit you can build.</li>
<li><strong>Post one piece of content daily.</strong> A Google Business Profile update, a social media post, or a quick video. Consistency compounds.</li>
<li><strong>Ask for one review per day.</strong> One review per day is 30 reviews per month. That's 360 reviews per year. Most of your competitors have fewer than 50.</li>
<li><strong>Check your numbers weekly.</strong> Cost per lead, cost per acquisition, close rate, and revenue by source. What gets measured gets managed.</li>
<li><strong>Follow up with every lead that doesn't convert.</strong> 80% of sales require 5 follow-ups, but 44% of contractors give up after one attempt. The fortune is in the follow-up.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Common Implementation Mistakes to Avoid</h3>

<p>Even with the right strategy, contractors make predictable mistakes during implementation:</p>

<p><strong>Mistake 1: Trying to do everything at once.</strong> You don't need all 40 strategies. Pick 3-5 that fit your budget and market. Master those before adding more.</p>

<p><strong>Mistake 2: Expecting instant results.</strong> SEO takes 3-6 months to show meaningful results. Content marketing takes 6-12 months. PPC works faster but still needs 30-60 days of optimization. Set realistic timelines.</p>

<p><strong>Mistake 3: Ignoring lead response speed.</strong> You can generate 100 leads per month, but if you call them back 4 hours later, you'll convert 10%. Call within 5 minutes and you'll convert 30-40%. The lead source matters less than your response speed.</p>

<p><strong>Mistake 4: Not tracking attribution.</strong> If you don't know which channel generated a lead, you can't optimize. Every lead should have a source, a cost, and a revenue outcome attached to it.</p>

<p><strong>Mistake 5: Giving up too soon.</strong> Most contractors abandon a strategy before it has time to work. They run PPC for two weeks, don't see results, and quit. They publish three blog posts, get no traffic, and stop. Marketing is a system that compounds. Give it time.</p>

